# 8. Better transmitter design

Represent the encoded message as:

```text
[
    DOT,
    DOT,
    DOT,
    CHARACTER_GAP,
    DASH,
    DASH,
    DASH,
    CHARACTER_GAP,
    DOT,
    DOT,
    DOT
]
```

Then your transmitter becomes deterministic:

```kotlin
enum class MorseSignal {
    DOT,
    DASH,
    CHARACTER_GAP,
    WORD_GAP
}
```

And:

```kotlin
suspend fun transmit(
    signals: List<MorseSignal>,
    unit: Long
) {
    for (signal in signals) {

        when (signal) {

            MorseSignal.DOT -> {
                flashlight.turnOn()
                delay(unit)
                flashlight.turnOff()
                delay(unit)
            }

            MorseSignal.DASH -> {
                flashlight.turnOn()
                delay(unit * 3)
                flashlight.turnOff()
                delay(unit)
            }

            MorseSignal.CHARACTER_GAP -> {
                delay(unit * 2)
            }

            MorseSignal.WORD_GAP -> {
                delay(unit * 6)
            }
        }
    }

    flashlight.turnOff()
}
```

Because the DOT/DASH already includes **1 unit of OFF time**, the additional gap becomes:

```text
Character gap:
3 total units
- 1 already provided
= 2 additional units
```

Likewise:

```text
Word gap:
7 total units
- 1 already provided
= 6 additional units
```

That's the timing model I'd use.

