## One architectural decision I'd make now

Don't name your core class something like:

```text
SOSFlashService
```

Make it generic:

```text
MorseTransmitter
```

Then:

```text
"SOS"
```

is just one input.

Later:

```text
"HELP"
"HELLO"
"I NEED HELP"
"12345"
```

can all use exactly the same engine.

So your fundamental architecture becomes:

```text
                 ┌──────────────┐
                 │  Compose UI  │
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        ↓
                 ┌──────────────┐
                 │   ViewModel  │
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        ↓
                 ┌──────────────┐
                 │ MorseEncoder │
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        ↓
                 ┌────────────────┐
                 │ MorseTransmitter│
                 └───────┬────────┘
                         ↓
                 ┌──────────────────┐
                 │ Flashlight       │
                 │ Controller       │
                 └────────┬─────────┘
                          ↓
                    Android Camera
                       Torch API
```

**That is the architecture I recommend you build.**

If you're starting from an empty Android Studio project, the next logical step is to set up the **Kotlin + Jetpack Compose project and implement the flashlight controller first**, then we can build the Morse encoder and transmitter on top of it.
